LLM ANSWER (verbatim):
The sketching approach uses a **partial program** as the starting point for synthesis. The programmer writes a “sketch,” which is a partial program that captures the **structure** or **implementation strategy** of the solution while leaving low-level details unspecified [1]. In JSKETCH’s description, such inputs may include **holes**, which are “unknown constants,” as well as **expression generators** and **class generators**; these are all ways of representing incomplete program parts that the synthesizer must fill in [4][5].

The role of the holes is to define a constrained search space. More generally, a sketch is described as a “program (or model) with holes,” and the sketch “constrains the space of possible programs the synthesizer needs to consider” [2][4][5]. The synthesizer then searches over the missing pieces and derives concrete values or code fragments that complete the program [1][4][5].

To make this work, the sketch is paired with **constraints/specifications**. In Solar-Lezama’s description, the programmer supplies either a **reference implementation** or **test routines** that the synthesized code must pass [1]. The paper also says the synthesizer can guarantee that a **test harness succeeds** for all values in a specified range [3]. So, the constraints serve as correctness criteria that the completed program must satisfy [1][3].

Putting it together: sketching works by combining  
1. a **partial program with holes** that encodes the intended structure, and  
2. **constraints** such as tests or a reference implementation that define what the completed program must satisfy [1][3][4][5].  
The synthesizer fills the holes while respecting those constraints [1][4][5].

What cannot be concluded from the passages alone: they do not fully specify the exact internal algorithm used to fill holes in the general case, beyond saying the synthesizer derives missing details and that the search is constrained by the sketch and correctness criteria [1][4][5].

